<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Apple requires this file, or App Store Connect rejects the build.

  What is actually collected, and why each entry below is what it is:

    - Precise location. The whole reason this app exists: the geofence engine
      clocks a worker in and out by where they are, continuously while
      tracking is on (see NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription in
      Info.plist and src/worker/location.ts). Linked to the worker's account
      because a position with nobody attached cannot produce a timesheet.
    - Photos. Site photos and receipt photos the worker takes or picks,
      uploaded to Supabase Storage against their account and the job. Linked
      for the same reason.
    - User ID. The Supabase auth user id every write is attributed to — RLS is
      keyed off it, so it is collected on every request, not stored
      separately for any secondary purpose.

  None of it feeds tracking as Apple defines the term: nothing here is used to
  link the worker's activity to advertising, sold or shared with a data
  broker, or joined with data from another company's app or site. There is no
  ad SDK in this project and nothing in NSPrivacyTrackingDomains — Crewline
  carries no ads. All three types exist solely for the app's own core
  function: recording a worker's hours and site records for their employer.

  NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes is empty because it should be, not because it was
  skipped: checked ios/Capacitor/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy and
  ios/CapacitorCordova/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy in @capacitor/ios (both declare
  no required-reason API usage), and grepped the Swift/Obj-C sources of
  @capacitor-community/background-geolocation and @capacitor/app — neither
  plugin has an xcprivacy of its own, and neither touches UserDefaults, file
  timestamps, system boot time, or disk space, the four categories the
  required-reason rule covers. The same grep against this app's own
  AppDelegate.swift and SceneDelegate.swift came back empty too. If a future
  dependency starts using one of those APIs, this array is where its reason
  code has to be added — Apple's static analysis at upload catches the gap
  either way, but better to be right before that stage.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhotosorVideos</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
